</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Assessing the reliability of Large Language Models (LLMs) by confidence elicitation is a prominent approach to AI safety in high-stakes applications, such as healthcare and finance.
Existing methods either require expensive computational overhead or suffer from poor calibration, making them impractical and unreliable for real-world deployment.
In this work, we propose GrACE, a Generative Approach to Confidence Elicitation that enables scalable and reliable confidence elicitation for LLMs.
GrACE adopts a novel mechanism in which the model expresses confidence by the similarity between the last hidden state and the embedding of a special token appended to the vocabulary, in real-time.
We fine-tune the model for calibrating the confidence with calibration targets associated with accuracy.
Experiments with three LLMs and two benchmark datasets show that the confidence produced by GrACE achieves the best discriminative capacity and calibration on open-ended generation tasks, outperforming six competing methods without resorting to additional sampling or an auxiliary model. Moreover, we propose two strategies for improving test-time scaling based on confidence induced by GrACE.
Experimental results show that using GrACE not only improves the accuracy of the final decision but also significantly reduces the number of required samples in the test-time scaling scheme, indicating the potential of GrACE as a practical solution for deploying LLMs with scalable, reliable, and real-time confidence estimation.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have become popular solutions to knowledge-intensive and logically complex problems for their capability of utilizing and reasoning over a huge amount of knowledge they learn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
However, the reliance on LLMs raises broad concerns about the possibility that LLMs output seemingly correct but factually hallucinated responses, especially in high-stakes domains such as healthcare <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and finance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Therefore, it is critical for LLMs to "know what they know" <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to become more reliable and gain users’ trust in their deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Estimating model confidence is a promising approach to assessing the reliability of model predictions.
A desirable confidence score should exhibit strong separability between positive and negative samples (discrimination) and align closely with actual correctness (calibration).
Although confidence elicitation and calibration have been extensively studied in classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, recent attention has shifted to open-ended generation tasks (e.g., question answering) for LLMs.
Nevertheless, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, existing methods exhibit limitations in <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when</span> a model expresses confidence and <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how well</span> that confidence is calibrated, which constrains their practical utility in applications such as Test-Time Scaling (TTS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
Post-generation methods, which assign the precise confidence score after generation, dominate current confidence elicitation approaches.
They elicit confidence either by prompting the original model again <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> or using auxiliary models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
Such methods require an additional confidence evaluation stage after generation, increasing latency and degrading human-computer interaction.
Verbalized confidence offers a generative alternative by producing numerical values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> or linguistic expressions (e.g.,most likely, likely, not sure) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> during the generation stage.
However, verbal expressions of confidence are inherently vague as the discrete vocabulary fails to capture nuance differences in confidence – for example, the confidence intervals for "most likely", "likely", and "unlikely" are neither standardized nor quantifiable.
Moreover, LLMs tend to be overconfident when expressing verbal confidence, potentially due to too much focus on imitating human expressions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09438v1/intro.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/181;" width="476" height="181" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison among the workflow and performance of post-generation methods, verbalized confidence, and GrACE for confidence elicitation.</span>
Post-generation methods require an additional evaluation step for calculating confidence and increase the latency in the inference stage.
Verbalized confidence methods generate verbal expressions about confidence along with the generation --- those are not calibrated.
GrACE generates calibrated confidence scores at generation time with negligible computational overhead (one more token for each generation)<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          We report time and Expected Calibration Error (ECE) using two representative methods in post-generation methods and verbalized confidence, namely <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">P</span>(True) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and verbalized confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
The experiment is conducted with the Llama2-7B model on the TriviaQA dataset, using a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU.</span></span></span>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we enable LLMs to learn the true distribution of the prediction correctness and express the confidence with <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>ene<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">r</span>ative c<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>librated <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>onfidence <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>licitation (GrACE), making it an efficient and reliable alternative for existing methods.
GrACE adds a special token <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> to the vocabulary and generates it at the end of the response for confidence elicitation.
Specifically, we fine-tune the model to express confidence using the similarity between the last hidden state and the embedding of <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span>, leveraging the inherent monotonicity and simplicity of the similarity function.
To ensure the elicited confidence is discriminative and calibrated, we construct a calibration set where training samples are grouped according to the internal confidence of the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and assigned group accuracy as the calibration target for fine-tuning.
To reduce the cost of fine-tuning, we only fine-tunes LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and the embedding of <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> when implementing GrACE.
The fine-tuning cost is amortised by the learned scalable confidence elicitation mechanism that generalizes across prompts and domains without an additional evaluation stage at test time.
The on-the-fly confidence elicitation mechanism, together with discriminative and reliable confidence scores, makes GrACE naturally well-suited for applications such as TTS, which demand dependable decision-making and real-time responses.
Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a generative confidence elicitation method GrACE, which provides a novel and scalable mechanism for discriminative and calibrated confidence elicitation on-the-fly with negligible computation overhead (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments over three backbone models and two benchmarks, in comparison to six existing methods, demonstrate GrACE outputs better confidence in the open-ended generation task.
GrACE surpasses the state-of-the-art method (Apricot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>) by 2.6%, 1.8%, and 4.4% in terms of ECE, Brier, and AUROC, respectively.
In addition, GrACE exhibits strong generalizability to unseen domains (§<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 GrACE for Open-ended Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We incorporate the real-time confidence provided by GrACE into the aggregation and adaptive sampling strategies in test-time scaling for improving the efficacy and efficiency.
Empirical results over two mainstream reasoning models show that GrACE improves the accuracy while greatly reducing the number of samples needed in test-time scaling (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 GrACE boosts Test-Time Scaling ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-generation Confidence Elicitation.</span>
Generative language models give their response towards prompts by sampling from the predictive probability.
Unlike model uncertainty, which captures the variability in a model’s responses to questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, confidence elicitation directly assigns an explicit confidence score to the model’s prediction.
For multiple choice questions where the model only generates a single token, the maximum softmax probability is shown to be aligned with correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
However, the maximum softmax probability becomes a less reliable indicator of correctness when the generation is open-ended.
Post-generation methods follow the generate-then-evaluate paradigm, which first acquires model generations, and then evaluates confidence score with an auxilarity model.
Kadavath et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> proposes <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">P</span>(True), which asks the model to self-evaluate its confidence on generation by converting the open-ended task to the multiple choice setting with careful prompting.
Further works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> calibrate <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">P</span>(True) by fine-tuning the model with LoRA using binary labeled data.
As for more efficient solutions
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, confidence can be detected from the model’s hidden state with a linear probe.
Post-generation methods are also applicable to black / gray-box models.
Ulmer et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> trains an auxiliary model with semantic-aware targets which predicts model confidence based on generation only.
GrACE differs from post-generation methods by eliminating the separate evaluation stage and producing the confidence score in the generation stage, thereby reducing computational overhead and accelerating the confidence elicitation process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verbalized Confidence Elicitation.</span>
Another branch of confidence elicitation aims to teach the models to express their confidence in natural language.
Tian et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> designs different prompting strategies to lead the model to output a calibrated confidence score.
Some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> endow the model with the ability to use confidence-related phrases (e.g., not sure, uncertain) to express confidence as humans do.
Cohen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> asks the model to generate a special token <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[idk]</span> when it feels uncertain about the responses.
Although verbalized confidence is a natural and efficient approach to eliciting model confidence, LLMs are found to be overconfident when verbalizing their confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Moreover, the confidence in the form of phrases conveys vague and unquantifiable information, restricting its application to broad topics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test-Time Scaling.</span>
Test-Time Scaling (TTS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> improves the response quality of LLMs with additional computing during the inference stage.
Repeated sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> with decision-making rules such as Self-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> is a common practice for selecting high-quality samples for given prompts.
However, this process requires a fixed sample size during TTS, which is not always necessary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
While some works investigate optimal computational resources allocation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, there is research focusing on dynamically adjusting the sample size.
Some attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> determine when to terminate sampling by pre-sampling and calculating statistical features within the pre-sample set.
Kim et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> applies the LLM-as-Judge technique <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> for evaluating the sample and process quality with reasoning models.
As a metric to evaluate the response quality, confidence is gradually applied to the decision-making process for improving TTS.
Taubenfeld et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and Huang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> optimize the sample size through the lens of confidence and calibration.
However, these works are based on <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">P</span>(True) which requires an additional evaluation stage as previously noted.
Concurrent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> leverages confidence to filter out low-quality reasoning traces but does not account for calibration, resulting in threshold selection without risk guarantees.
Our work enables on-the-fly elicitation of discriminative and well-calibrated confidence, supporting efficient, effective, and risk-controlled TTS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the formulation of the generative confidence elicitation problem in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and the GrACE framework in §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, including the form of confidence elicitation in GrACE §<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Confidence Elicitation in GrACE ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>, the calibration target §<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Setting Target Confidence for GrACE ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>, training process §<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 Training Process ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a>, and GrACE’s application on test-time scaling §<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS4" title="3.2.4 Test-Time Scaling with GrACE ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and a model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the generative confidence (GenConf) <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{y}_{i},{\bm{z}},c_{i})=\mathcal{M}(\bm{x}),\ c_{\text{i}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{+}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{y}_{i},{\bm{z}},c_{i})=\mathcal{M}(\bm{x}),\ c_{\text{i}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}=\{y_{i}^{1},...,y_{i}^{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}=\{y_{i}^{1},...,y_{i}^{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}=\{z_{1},...,z_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}=\{z_{1},...,z_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s hidden state, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the confidence score corresponding to the sample <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that Eq.<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> requires no auxiliary model and the confidence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> should be a real number extracted during generation rather than a textual string.
To be effective and trustworthy in downstream applications, the GenConf <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> should exhibit both discrimination and calibration.
Formally:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">Discrimination:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\mathbb{P}(c_{m}&gt;c_{n}|f(\bm{y}_{m})=1,f(\bm{y}_{n})=0)=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\mathbb{P}(c_{m}&gt;c_{n}|f(\bm{y}_{m})=1,f(\bm{y}_{n})=0)=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">Calibration:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{y}_{i})=1|c_{i}=p)=p," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{y}_{i})=1|c_{i}=p)=p,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a function which assigns 1 to the correct response and 0 to the wrong response.
Given these notions, discrimination requires
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to correctly rank positive samples above negative ones, whereas calibration requires <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to reflect the actual accuracy of the model’s responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>GrACE</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe the GrACE framework (illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2.1 Confidence Elicitation in GrACE ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) for teaching the model to produce GenConf in this section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Confidence Elicitation in GrACE</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ideally, the confidence should be expressed in continuous space during generation as proposed in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Inspired by the pause token mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> which prompts the model to "think" by generating a sequence of special, non-semantic tokens before reasoning, we introduce a new token, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span>, into the vocabulary to encourage the reflective behavior of the model in the latent space.
Specifically, we append <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> to the end of each question-answer pair.
An example of the revised question answering data is shown below:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.SS2.SSS1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:35%;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="100.19" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 167.08 100.19" width="167.08"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,100.19) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 94.28 C 0 97.55 2.64 100.19 5.91 100.19 L 161.18 100.19 C 164.44 100.19 167.08 97.55 167.08 94.28 L 167.08 5.91 C 167.08 2.64 164.44 0 161.18 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBF0FA;" fill="#EBF0FA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 94.28 C 1.97 96.46 3.73 98.22 5.91 98.22 L 161.18 98.22 C 163.35 98.22 165.12 96.46 165.12 94.28 L 165.12 5.91 C 165.12 3.73 163.35 1.97 161.18 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 17.72 12.35)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.51em;--ltx-fo-height:5.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.04em;font-size:10pt;" height="76.57" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.03)" width="131.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:9.51em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question: Who found the remains of the Titanic?

<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Answer: Bob Ballard <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We train the model to generate <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> at the end of its response, thereby enabling an additional forward pass in which the model can reflect on its output and estimate confidence in the latent space.
In what follows, we describe how the model expresses its confidence in the latent space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{e}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒆</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{e}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the embedding of the new token <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}</annotation></semantics></math> be hidden states in the last layer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span>.
Then, the confidence is estimated as the similarity between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{e}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒆</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{e}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\mathrm{sim}(\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}},\bm{e}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒆</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\mathrm{sim}(\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}},\bm{e}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In causal language models, the language modeling head (typically a linear projection followed by a softmax) serves as a mechanism to project the last hidden state to the vocabulary.
This projection implicitly evaluates the similarity between the last hidden state and each token embedding in the vocabulary.
We leverage this built-in capability as the foundation for defining function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sim}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sim}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning it with the model’s native representation geometry:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{E}\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}})_{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{E}\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}})_{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{(|\mathcal{V}|+1)\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{(|\mathcal{V}|+1)\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding matrix of the vocabulary, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of token embeddings, the subscript <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> is the position associated with <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span>.
Note that the position of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> is flexible depending on the segment that the users care about, we provide the experiment results with <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> prepending the response in Ablation Study in §<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 GrACE for Open-ended Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09438v1/method.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:550/300;" width="550" height="300" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The illustration of GrACE framework.</span>
We group the calibration data using the model’s self-awareness and use the accuracy of each group as the calibration target (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Setting Target Confidence for GrACE ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>).
The model is trained with the combination of calibration loss and supervised fine-tuning loss (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 Training Process ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a>).
The confidence is elicited by the distance between the hidden state and <span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> embedding (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Confidence Elicitation in GrACE ‣ 3.2 GrACE ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>).
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Setting Target Confidence for GrACE</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To guide the model to generate a discriminative and well-calibrated confidence score <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, it is important to set target confidences for responses <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> that are distributionally sharp and numerically aligned with their actual probability of correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
Traditional methods use binary labels as confidence targets, leading to only a sharp distribution with poor calibration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
We propose to smooth the confidence target and ensure calibration by grouping the training data and assigning the empirical accuracy within each group as the supervision target.
The grouping is determined by the outputs of a linear probe trained with binary labels, which helps ensure a sharper distribution.
Specifically, we take the question-answer pair <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> as input to the model and probe an uncalibrated confidence score <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> from the last hidden layer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of the model for generation in sequence length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> with a linear probe parameterized with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s={\bm{W}}_{\theta}^{T}\bar{\bm{z}}_{L}+{\bm{b}}_{\theta},\quad\bar{\bm{z}}_{L}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bm{z}^{i}_{L}}{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s={\bm{W}}_{\theta}^{T}\bar{\bm{z}}_{L}+{\bm{b}}_{\theta},\quad\bar{\bm{z}}_{L}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bm{z}^{i}_{L}}{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We perform k-fold binning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> to align <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> from probing with response accuracy for every training sample.
Specifically, we partition the dataset into <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> folds, iteratively train the linear classifier on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> folds and test on the remaining fold until all the samples in the training data <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> are annotated with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
To convert <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to response accuracy, we group the data into bins with equal intervals according to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and calculate the accuracy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{A}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>A</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{A}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in each bin <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let the target <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if the data point <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\in B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\in B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting calibration set is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{c}=\left\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i},t_{i})\right\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{c}=\left\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i},t_{i})\right\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In this way, we obtain targets for GrACE that are numerically equal to the accuracies within each group and sharply distributed, as the probe is trained to assess the factuality of the question–answer pairs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Training Process</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We optimize the model to align its self-reflective confidence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> with the calibration target <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> using the Mean Squared Error (MSE) as calibration loss.
However, fine-tuning the model towards only the calibration objective will sacrifice the model’s overall performance.
To maintain the model utility, we adopt the supervised fine-tuning (SFT) loss on the original generation of the model as a regularizer.
Note that we exclude the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> from the calculation of SFT loss to avoid the conflict between the two objectives.
The total loss is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{T}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{C}}+\gamma\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}(t_{i}-\mathrm{sim}(\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}},e^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}))^{2}-\gamma\mathrm{log}p_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>C</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;CNF&gt;</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{T}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{C}}+\gamma\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}(t_{i}-\mathrm{sim}(\bm{z}_{L}^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}},e^{\texttt{&lt;CNF&gt;}}))^{2}-\gamma\mathrm{log}p_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter for balancing the loss terms <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>C</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Training the model to achieve calibration amortizes the computation cost at the inference stage and is more effective compared with the black-box methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4 </span>Test-Time Scaling with GrACE</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, GrACE helps the model to output discriminative and well-calibrated confidence with negligible inference cost.
Building upon this, we incorporate GrACE into the common aggregation and adaptive sampling practices, namely self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and early-stopping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, in TTS for improving the scaling law at inference time without much additional computation for confidence estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Consistency with GrACE (GrACE-SC).</span>
Self-consistency determines the accepted response by taking a majority vote over all samples generated through TTS.
Intuitively, given the variance in sample quality, the voting process should account for this heterogeneity by assigning greater weight to samples with higher confidence.
Thus, we leverage the discrimination of the confidence and reformulate the self-consistency.
Given the sample answer set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{a_{1},...,a_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{a_{1},...,a_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where the answer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the final answer extracted from model response <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the sampling budget, GrACE-SC determines the final answer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{sc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{sc}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{sc}=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}}\sum_{i=1}^{T}\mathbb{I}(a_{i}=a)\cdot c_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{sc}=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}}\sum_{i=1}^{T}\mathbb{I}(a_{i}=a)\cdot c_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is an indicator function which returns 1 if the answer extracted from the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matches <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> else returns 0.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:125.8pt;vertical-align:-61.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-209.7pt,76.5pt) scale(0.451340185663764,0.451340185663764) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_ttt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_ttt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_ttt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TriviaQA</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_ttt" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SciQ</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Backbone</span></td>
