</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose GrACE, an approach to eliciting calibrated generative confidence from LLMs.
Unlike post-generation and verbalized confidence elicitation methods, GrACE helps the model to output a confidence score using the real number during generation.
The LLM is trained to express its confidence by internally measuring the similarity between its last hidden state and the embedding of a special token <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span>.
GrACE first constructs calibration targets by probing the self-awareness of the model towards the facuality of the generation and connects the self-awareness to accuracy using k-fold binning.
The model is then fine-tuned to assign a probability mass that correlates with calibration targets to the <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;CNF&gt;</span> at the end of its response.
Extensive experiments show that GrACE outperforms six confidence elicitation methods in terms of open-ended generation calibration, while demonstrating strong domain generalization ability.
Moreover, the calibrated confidence score from the model trained with GrACE improves both the efficacy and efficiency of test-time scaling.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
